-- S23 Maharashtra prose — mahad-raigad (Varadavinayak)
-- Verdict matrix: 6 go / 3 skip / 3 wait
-- Anchors: 1725 Peshwa Subhedar Ramji Mahadev Bivalkar restoration; Varada = boon-granter;
--          sweet-water Kuthar Lake adjoins temple; Raigad Fort 30km (Shivaji's capital, 1674 coronation);
--          7th in Ashtavinayak yatra protocol; Konkan/Raigad 3500-4000mm monsoon;
--          Ganesh Chaturthi Sep 12 + Anant Chaturdashi Sep 22; Maghi Ganesh Jayanti Feb 4 2026.

INSERT INTO destination_months (
  destination_id, month, verdict, score, prose_lead, why_go, why_not
) VALUES
('mahad-raigad', 1, 'go', 4, 'Mahad sits in the Khalapur taluka of Raigad, an 87km drive south of Pune via the Mumbai-Pune Expressway and the Khopoli exit, with the temple complex 4km off the highway beside the sweet-water Kuthar Lake. January gives the Konkan its cleanest air — 18-28C, low haze, dry pavements — and Varadavinayak (the boon-granting Ganesh) draws a steady but unhurried weekend flow. The 1725 Peshwa-era Subhedar Ramji Mahadev Bivalkar restoration sits on what locals call a cave-temple origin under a banyan; the present square sanctum is small enough to feel intimate during a 6am abhishek slot. The lake adjacent is fed by hill springs and stays sweet through the dry season — ritual snan happens at the eastern ghat. Raigad Fort (Shivaji''s capital, 1674 coronation) is 30km southwest; pilgrims often combine the two in a single Raigad-day itinerary.', 'Mild Konkan winter, calm post-festival temple, and the option to combine with Raigad Fort 30km away make January the most comfortable single-temple day-trip month before the Magh Ganesh Jayanti wave.', NULL),
('mahad-raigad', 2, 'go', 5, 'February is Mahad''s second-peak month after September. Maghi Ganesh Jayanti falls Wed Feb 4 2026 — the Magh Shukla Chaturthi anniversary when every Ashtavinayak runs a 3-day mahotsav. Varadavinayak''s Feb mahotsav is among the quieter of the 8 (Pali and Theur draw bigger numbers) but the temple''s small scale means even modest crowds fill the courtyard. Palki procession leaves the temple Feb 4 evening; abhishek at 4am-7am. The Kuthar Lake stays cool with hill-spring inflow and morning bathers gather on the east ghat by 5:30am. Weather holds 18-30C with no humidity yet. Outside Feb 2-6 the temple is back to weekend-flow normal. Yatra package operators running 2-night/3-day Pune circuits routinely fit Mahad as their day-2 Konkan stop — book Mahad lodging at least 14 days ahead for the Feb 2-6 window.', 'Maghi Ganesh Jayanti Feb 4 2026 brings the small-but-intense secondary mahotsav with palki, dawn abhishek, and Kuthar Lake ritual snan; weather still in clean Konkan winter mode.', NULL),
('mahad-raigad', 3, 'go', 4, 'March is the last clean Konkan month before pre-monsoon humidity sets in. Daytime climbs 30-34C but mornings 5-9am hold at 20-22C. The temple complex is quiet — no major March Ganesh anchor — and the 1725 Bivalkar stonework photographs cleanly in dry light. Gudi Padwa (Marathi New Year) falls Mar 19 2026; weekend regional pilgrim flow lifts Mar 18-22 for first-darshan rituals but Mahad''s Raigad location stays calmer than Pune-cluster temples. The Kuthar Lake level drops noticeably through March as the dry-season minimum approaches. This is the best month for a leisurely Raigad-Mahad combined trip: morning Varadavinayak darshan, midday drive to Raigad Fort (30km, takes 1h), Pratapgad ropeway or Maha-Darwaza ascent in afternoon, return overnight. Avoid Mar 18-22 if seeking solitude.', 'Last clean Konkan window before humidity loads in; Gudi Padwa Mar 19 brings Marathi New Year flow Mar 18-22 but the rest of March is ideal for combined Mahad-Raigad-Fort day-trip itineraries.', NULL),
('mahad-raigad', 4, 'wait', 3, 'April brings Konkan humidity head-on. Daytime 32-36C with 70-80% humidity makes pavement pradakshina and open-courtyard waiting fatiguing by 9:30am. The temple stays open through normal hours but most yatra packages skip April entirely. Mornings 5-8am are still workable for a single-temple day-trip if you commit to a 4am Pune start. Kuthar Lake level is at its annual minimum; the eastern ghat ritual snan platform is exposed and the hill-spring inflow is reduced. Raigad Fort sees stronger April footfall from Shivaji-history pilgrims (Hindavi Swarajya commemoration around Apr 6, Shiv Jayanti anniversary) than the temple itself. Combining the two in April requires accepting heat-fatigue on the fort climb. Lodging in Mahad and Khalapur drops weekend rates 30-40%.', 'Mornings 5-8am remain workable for single-temple Varadavinayak darshan; Mahad lodging at annual lows and Raigad Fort''s Shiv Jayanti period (early April) makes a tight one-day combined trip feasible if you start at 4am.', 'After 9:30am the 32-36C daytime with 70-80% Konkan humidity makes any open-courtyard waiting or Kuthar Lake ghat time genuinely fatiguing; full multi-temple yatra logistics break down.'),
('mahad-raigad', 5, 'skip', 2, 'May is the wrong month for Mahad. Konkan humidity peaks 80-85% with 34-38C heat; the open-sky prakaram pavements stay too hot for barefoot pradakshina from 9:30am until 5pm. The temple stays operational but darshan timings shift to early-morning and post-7pm windows only for genuine comfort. Akshaya Tritiya falls May 1 2026 — a Marathi anchor that lifts numbers briefly. Mumbai weekend pilgrims still attempt single-temple visits but most Ashtavinayak yatra package operators close their May circuits entirely. The Kuthar Lake is at its annual low; some years the eastern ghat is unusable due to weed bloom. Pre-monsoon haze kills photography. Wait for October''s post-monsoon clarity or September''s festival peak.', NULL, 'Konkan humidity 80-85% combined with 34-38C peak heat makes Mahad''s open prakaram and Kuthar Lake ghat genuinely difficult between 9:30am and 5pm; yatra packages close the May circuit entirely.'),
('mahad-raigad', 6, 'wait', 3, 'June marks Konkan monsoon onset. First 7-10 days are usually false-start showers; by Jun 10-15 the southwest front consolidates and Raigad enters its 3500-4000mm wet season. The Mumbai-Pune Expressway stays open year-round, but Khopoli-Mahad state-highway sections see waterfalls cascading onto road, periodic mudslides, and 30-60 minute closures. Konkan ferry routes from Mumbai-side (Mandwa, Alibaug, Murud) start curtailing late June — affects multi-stop yatra connections. The temple itself never closes through monsoon. Kuthar Lake begins refilling rapidly; by Jun 25 it''s back to high level. Early June (1-10) is workable if showers stay light. Combining Mahad with Raigad Fort gets dangerous — fort''s 1737 steps become slick and the ropeway closes in heavy rain.', 'Early June (1-10) before monsoon consolidation still allows single-temple Mahad day-trips; the Kuthar Lake landscape transforms green and crowds drop sharply.', 'After Jun 15 the southwest monsoon consolidates over Raigad; Khopoli-Mahad highway closures and Raigad Fort step-slick conditions break the combined fort-temple itinerary entirely.'),
('mahad-raigad', 7, 'skip', 2, 'July is peak Konkan monsoon over Raigad. Mahad sees 900-1100mm of rainfall in the single month — among the heaviest single-month totals on the central Konkan stretch. State highway closures of 1-3 days occur during the heaviest spells when river crossings overtop. The Mumbai-Pune Expressway sees aquaplaning advisories and fog on the Khandala-Lonavala descent. Konkan ferry routes from the Mumbai-side are off entirely through July. The Kuthar Lake adjoining the temple overflows — the eastern ghat ritual snan platform sits submerged. Raigad Fort''s 1737-step climb is closed to general visitors during heavy rain, killing the combined fort-and-temple itinerary that drives most non-yatra Mahad visits. Ashadhi-Ekadashi (Jul 4 2026, Pandharpur Wari) pulls Marathi devotional flow south toward Pandharpur, not Raigad Ashtavinayak. The temple stays open but darshan numbers fall to monthly lows. Wait for September peak or October clarity.', NULL, 'July 900-1100mm Mahad rainfall triggers ghat closures, expressway aquaplaning, fort step shutdowns, and ferry suspension; reaching the temple reliably is the problem, not the temple itself.'),
('mahad-raigad', 8, 'skip', 2, 'August continues heavy southwest monsoon over the central Konkan and Raigad. Rainfall holds 700-900mm for the month — still well above any threshold for casual visitor logistics. Sankashti Chaturthi falls Tue Aug 4 2026 (Angarki Chaturthi — Tuesday-aligned auspicious fast day) and brings a dedicated Ganesh-devotee pilgrim flow despite weather, with palki at dawn, abhishek through the day, and modak prasad in the evening. Beyond Aug 4 the month is quiet. Konkan logistics stay broken: Mumbai-side ferries off entirely, periodic ghat closures on the Khopoli-Mahad state highway, and Raigad Fort closed to general visitors. Hardcore Ashtavinayak devotees do undertake the monsoon yatra as a tapas (austerity) vow — Mahad''s small 1725 Bivalkar Peshwa-era stonework against the green Raigad hills and the overflowing Kuthar Lake is visually remarkable — but for general visitors the access risk and physical wet-weather discomfort make it unwise. Wait for September festival peak.', NULL, 'August continues 700-900mm Raigad rainfall with ghat closures, ferry suspension, and Raigad Fort access shut; only the Aug 4 Angarki Chaturthi pulls dedicated devotees who accept weather as part of the vow.'),
('mahad-raigad', 9, 'go', 5, 'September is Mahad''s absolute peak. Ganesh Chaturthi falls Sat Sep 12 2026 opening the 10-day Ganeshotsav closing Anant Chaturdashi Tue Sep 22. Mahad sees its highest annual footfall: 25,000-40,000 visitors on the Sep 12 chaturthi day, palki processions from surrounding hamlets, near-continuous abhishek, 16-hour darshan, and bhajan-sandhya programmes through evenings. Ashtavinayak yatra packages run continuously — MTDC, MSRTC, and 30+ private operators put Mahad on day-2 of standard Pune-Konkan 2-night/3-day circuits (₹3500-8000 per pilgrim). The monsoon is in tail phase — Sep 1-15 still wet, Sep 16-30 drying — but Raigad green is at peak. Kuthar Lake is full. Book lodging at least 90 days ahead; budget 4-5 hour darshan queues on chaturthi day.', 'Ganesh Chaturthi Sep 12 to Anant Chaturdashi Sep 22 is the annual Ashtavinayak peak with 25,000-40,000 chaturthi-day visitors, continuous abhishek, Kuthar Lake at full level, and full yatra-package machinery in operation.', NULL),
('mahad-raigad', 10, 'go', 5, 'October is the post-monsoon clarity window. Konkan dries up after Anant Chaturdashi; by Oct 10 rain stops, Raigad green peaks visually, humidity drops to 60-65%, and daytime settles at 28-32C. Temple traffic stays moderate through Oct 15 from September overflow, then thins. Sharad Purnima (Kojagiri) falls Oct 25 2026 — Marathi anchor that lifts numbers briefly with all-night bhajan programmes. The post-monsoon Raigad landscape — washed basalt, paddy ready for harvest, Western Ghats views unobstructed — makes October the best photography month. Kuthar Lake stays full from monsoon refill. The Mahad-Raigad Fort combined day-trip works perfectly: morning Varadavinayak, midday fort climb (now safe and dry), evening return. Ferries restart fully by Oct 15.', 'Post-monsoon clarity peaks with washed Raigad basalt, harvest paddy, full Kuthar Lake, and Raigad Fort safely re-opened — the single best month for the combined Mahad-Varadavinayak plus Shivaji-capital itinerary.', NULL),
('mahad-raigad', 11, 'go', 5, 'November is Mahad''s most comfortable month. Daytime 26-30C, nights 18-22C, near-zero humidity, glass-clear post-monsoon air. Tulsi Vivah (Nov 1 2026) and Kartik Purnima (Nov 24) anchor the Marathi devotional calendar — both bring moderate pilgrim flow without September-scale crush. Pune-Mumbai weekend day-trippers fill the temple Sat-Sun; weekday mornings 6-10am are calm. Yatra package operators run a secondary high season Nov 15-Dec 15 with Mahad on day-2 of standard circuits — book lodging 3 weeks ahead. The combined Raigad Fort visit is at its visual peak: low humidity gives unbroken views from Bale-Killa toward Mahabaleshwar and the Mahad valley. Kuthar Lake holds clean water, attracts migratory waterbirds (lesser whistling duck, river tern). The locals-recommend month over September for non-festival pilgrims.', 'Clearest air, lowest humidity, full Kuthar Lake with waterbirds, and ideal Raigad Fort visibility — the locals-recommended Ashtavinayak month for pilgrims wanting depth without September crowds.', NULL),
('mahad-raigad', 12, 'go', 4, 'December stays mild on the Konkan — 24-30C daytime, 16-20C nights. Year-end Pune-Mumbai escape tourism peaks; yatra package buses run Dec 20-Jan 5 at near-September density. Angarki Chaturthi falls Tue Dec 1 — auspicious Tuesday fast day with palki ceremony at dawn. Margashirsha Purnima (Dec 24 2026) is a minor anchor. Weather is reliable; temple campus stays in its post-monsoon clean state. Kuthar Lake holds steady levels through winter. Christmas-New Year week (Dec 25-Jan 2) sees Mumbai weekend day-trippers stretch lunch crowds at the trust prasad-bhandar. Off-peak mid-December (Dec 5-15) is closer to November-quiet. Combined Raigad Fort visit remains ideal — winter clarity holds. Book Mahad lodging 30 days ahead for the Dec 20-Jan 5 stretch; mid-month is walk-in friendly.', 'Mild Konkan winter, Angarki Chaturthi Dec 1, and a secondary yatra package season Dec 20-Jan 5; off-peak mid-December stays close to November-quiet with Raigad Fort visibility still at peak.', NULL)
ON CONFLICT (destination_id, month) DO UPDATE SET
  verdict = EXCLUDED.verdict,
  score = EXCLUDED.score,
  prose_lead = EXCLUDED.prose_lead,
  why_go = EXCLUDED.why_go,
